 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to increasing the\ntotal amount of bonds the Buffalo sewer authority may issue",
    "basePrintNo": "S6292",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6292-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1187, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6292\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to increasing\nthe total amount of bonds the Buffalo sewer authority may issue\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to allow the Buffalo Sewer Authority to have\nbonds issued and outstanding at any one time shall not exceed in the\naggregate the sum of five hundred million dollars.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 provides that the Buffalo Sewer Authority may have bonds\nissued and outstanding at any one time not exceed in the aggregate the\nsum of $500 million dollars, excluding bonds or notes for the payment or\nredemption of which there has been set aside and held in trust either\nmoneys, or direct and general obligations of, or obligations guaranteed\nby, the United States of America, or obligations secured by such obli-\ngations, or any combination thereof, which are or will be sufficient to\npay when due the principal or applicable redemption price and all\naccrued interest thereon and, if such bonds or notes are to be redeemed,\nfor which notice of redemption has been given or satisfactory provision\nhas been made for the giving of such notice.\n\nSection 2 provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Buffalo Sewer Authority protects public health and the Great Lakes\nwaterways, while striving to maintain affordability of its services and\nbuild greater equity in Western New York. The Authority's State Pollu-\ntant Discharge Elimination System permit authorized the implementation\nof an abatement plan to address the City's stormwater challenge that\nresults in sewer overflows to area waterways. In partnership with the US\nEnvironmental Protection Agency and NYS Department of Environmental\nConservation, the Authority developed a Long-Term Control Plan (LTCP),\nthe enforcement of which is governed by a federal Administrative Order.\n\nIn December 2020, the Authority had a Financial Capability Assessment\n(FCA) completed to help plan for future funding of the remaining work to\nbe done under the LTCP, as well as other Authority operations. Due to\nunforeseen challenges in implementing certain components of the LTCP,\nthe estimated cost to complete it has greatly escalated.\n\nWhereas the cost to complete the LTCP was initially estimated to be $380\nmillion in 2014, the FCA shows that the Authority will need an addi-\ntional $537 million in 2019 dollars to fulfill its LTCP requirements.\nThe Authority estimates it will have an annual average of about $4.5\nmillion over the next five years available as pay-as-you-go funding for\nall capital projects, inclusive of those required under the LTCP. This\nprojection accounts for an 11¤/o decrease in revenue the Authority has\nexperienced due to the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\nThe Authority acknowledges and appreciates that the State increased the\nAuthority's bond limit to $250 million in 2018. However, the updated FCA\nshows that the Authority will need this debt limit doubled to meet its\nobligations under the LTCP. Furthermore, given current historically low\ninterest rates and limited anticipated pay-as-you go funding in the\ncoming years, the Authority believes it most prudent to finance LTCP\nprojects with bonds. Therefore, it is necessary to amend Section 1187 of\nthe Public Authorities Law so that the total amount of bonds issued and\noutstanding at any one time shall not exceed $500 million, rather than\nthe current limit of $250 million.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA7068/S6769 of 2021/2022\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
